                <h2>A Monday Morning That Made the Team Panic</h2>
<p>Last month, Lao Zhou, head of a cross-border beauty brand team, was furious at the weekly meeting. They had launched a new multi-functional eyebrow pencil on TikTok Shop, running three ad creatives in the first week with an $800 budget—but only generated $240 in GMV, a ROI of just 0.3.</p>
<p>"We tested the creatives, spent the money, and watched it disappear like water down the drain." Lao Zhou sighed, scrolling through the backend data. What frustrated him more: the team had only two operations staff, juggling store management and content creation, with no bandwidth for systematic planning.</p>
<p>This is a typical cold-start dilemma many overseas teams face: product ready, ad account set up, budget approved—but the creative pool is empty. Or worse, filled with "fill-in" content that doesn't perform.</p>
<h2>Diagnosis: Three Fatal Weaknesses in a Thin Creative Pool</h2>
<p>After reviewing Lao Zhou's case, we identified three core problems:</p>
<p><strong>1. Single Creative Type, All Product Showcases</strong></p>
<p>The team created content with a "traditional e-commerce mindset"—product close-ups with subtitle narration. A 15-second video showing the eyebrow pencil rotating twice. No usage scenarios, no before-and-after comparisons, no real people. TikTok users swipe away instantly.</p>
<p><strong>2. No Layered Testing, All Eggs in One Basket</strong></p>
<p>They stacked the entire budget on one "best-looking" creative without any A/B testing. When that creative failed, the entire campaign crashed.</p>
<p><strong>3. Severe Lack of UGC Content</strong></p>
<p>Beauty products on TikTok thrive on "product discovery," and discovery often starts with authentic user sharing. Lao Zhou's team had zero UGC or light KOC content, completely missing out on TikTok's traffic dividends.</p>
<h2>Actions: A Four-Week Creative Pool Build SOP</h2>
<p>We designed a four-week creative pool strategy with the core principle: "Build the pool first, then stratify, then iterate continuously."</p>
<h3>Week 1: Creative Type Matrix</h3>
<p>First, define the creative type structure for TikTok Shop beauty launches:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Type</th>
<th>Ratio</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Product Showcase</td>
<td>20%</td>
<td>Basic reach</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Usage Scenario</td>
<td>30%</td>
<td>Discovery conversion</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Before/After Comparison</td>
<td>25%</td>
<td>Trust building</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>UGC/Amateur Testimonial</td>
<td>25%</td>
<td>Broad reach</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Lao Zhou's team allocated production tasks according to this ratio, prioritizing usage scenarios and comparison content. By the weekend, they completed the first batch of 12 creatives, covering real scenarios like morning commute makeup and date-night looks.</p>
<h3>Week 2: Small-Budget Layered Testing</h3>
<p>With creatives ready, Week 2 began small-budget layered testing. We suggested five ad sets at $30-50/day each, testing different creative types and audience targeting combinations.</p>
<p><strong>Testing Cadence</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Days 1-2: Observe CTR and CPC</li>
<li>Days 3-4: Review CPM and page dwell time</li>
<li>Days 5-7: Rank by ROAS, eliminate bottom performers</li>
</ul>
<p>By week's end, usage scenario creatives hit a ROAS of 1.8, far outperforming the product showcase at 0.6. The team immediately scaled winning creatives and shut down underperformers.</p>
<h3>Week 3: UGC Integration and Remixing</h3>
<p>After identifying top-performing directions, the team introduced UGC content. Operations staff searched TikTok for popular topics in their category, found a few viral amateur videos with strong natural engagement, obtained permissions, and created secondary remixes.</p>
<p><strong>Remix technique</strong>: Keep original audio and core selling points, add brand hooks in the first 3 seconds (text effects or voiceover guidance), keep total duration at 12-15 seconds.</p>
<p>These UGC remixes launched with strong authenticity, CPM dropped 40% compared to self-produced content, while conversion rate increased 1.5x.</p>
<h3>Week 4: Data Review and Creative Library Documentation</h3>
<p>By the end of four weeks, the team conducted a systematic review of all creatives, building an effectiveness dashboard. They tagged creatives by type, duration, hook format, and other dimensions, recording lifecycle data for each.</p>
<p>Lao Zhou said at the review meeting: "Before, we'd use a creative and forget it. Now we finally have our own creative library."</p>
<h2>Results: ROI Recovery from 0.3 to 3.2</h2>
<p>After the four-week cold start, this multi-functional eyebrow pencil broke $100K in monthly GMV, with stable ad ROI at 3.2—nearly a 10x improvement from the initial 0.3.</p>
<p>More importantly, the creative pool expanded from 3 to 45 reusable creatives, with 30% reaching "viral" status (ROAS &gt; 4), building sustainable content assets.</p>
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>The core of TikTok Shop cold launches isn't about "spending money"—it's about "building the pool." Creative pool depth determines testing efficiency and流量承接能力. If you're struggling with cold starts, ask yourself three questions:</p>
<ol>
<li>Are my creative types diverse enough?</li>
<li>Am I running small-budget layered tests?</li>
<li>Is UGC included in my content planning?</li>
</ol>
<p>Answer these three clearly, and you've already succeeded halfway.</p>
